<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pannonia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pannonia is a historical region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, covering parts of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, originally a Roman province. In the late eighth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> campaigned there against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>. Its conquest extended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>'s south-eastern frontier.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pannonia.jpg" alt="Pannonia" width="320" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WikiEditor2004 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pannonia01b.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pannonia was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> bounded on the north and east by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, on the west by Noricum and upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and on the south by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>. It included western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>. In the Early Iron Age Transdanubia was inhabited by the Pannonians, a collection of Illyrian tribes. Celtic groups, the Scordisci, the Boii and others, later held much of the region, and the Dacian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burebista">Burebista</a> overcame several of them between 65 and 50 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s advance began with the foundation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> in 181 BCE. In 35 BCE Octavian captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisak">Siscia</a> after a month-long siege and occupied much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> valley. In 15 BCE the future emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> defeated the Scordisci, and his operations of 12 to 9 BCE almost certainly brought the whole of Transdanubia under Roman control. Augustus later claimed in his Res Gestae that Tiberius had brought the Pannonian peoples under Roman authority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-illyrian-revolt">Bellum Batonianum</a>, a revolt led by the Breuci under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bato-the-breucian">Bato the Breucian</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daesitiates">Daesitiates</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bato-the-daesitiate">Bato the Daesitiate</a>, lasted three years until the last rebel laid down arms in 9 CE. Illyricum was then divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and Pannonia.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> finished the occupation and began building the frontier, the limes. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>, between 103 and 107, the province was divided into Pannonia Inferior and Pannonia Superior, the latter with most of the urbanised areas and three legions. Wars with the Marcomanni and Quadi under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>, from 166, brought heavy destruction and loss of life to Pannonia. Diocletian and his successors divided it into four provinces, Prima, Valeria, Savia and Secunda. In 324 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> annexed the plains to the east up to a new limes, the Devil's Dykes.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> lost the province in stages. In 433 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Flavius Aetius</a> ceded part of it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, and after the Hunnic empire collapsed in 454, Emperor Marcian settled large numbers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> there as foederati. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>'s Eastern Roman Empire held southern parts in the 6th century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> invaded in the 560s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> settled over time. In the 790s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> invaded and used the name Pannonia for a new frontier province, the March of Pannonia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Roman Pannonia was fairly productive, with oats and barley its chief crops, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> an important export until the forests were cleared. Native settlements were organised in cantons, while most large towns were of Roman origin, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnuntum">Carnuntum</a>, Vindobona, Aquincum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savaria">Savaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisak">Siscia</a>. Christianity began to spread in the province in the 2nd century. A dialect called Pannonian Latin developed and went extinct around the 6th century. The ancient name survives in the modern term Pannonian plain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pannonia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 568 the Lombards left <b>Pannonia</b> for Italy, and the Avars took over the whole basin, the great plain enclosed by the Carpathians and the Alps.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Avar campaigns of the 790s destroyed the power of the Avar khaganate in <b>Pannonia</b> and brought back so much treasure, reportedly fifteen wagons, each drawn by four oxen, that it transformed Carolingian finances.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The first named ruler is Mojmír I, “duke of the Moravians”, who according to the Conversio Bagoariorum et Carantanorum expelled Pribina across the Danube; Pribina fled to Ratpot, who administered the March of <b>Pannonia</b> from around 833.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ellak, his favoured son and ruler of the Akatziri, met the Gepid king Ardaric's coalition at the Battle of Nedao, around 454, and the Amali Goths under Valamir revolted the same year; many Germanic vassals were lost and the Huns were driven from <b>Pannonia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Marcomannic Wars some Marcomanni were settled in Italy and other parts of the empire, Gallienus settled others in <b>Pannonia</b> Superior, and by the late 4th century at least some had become Christians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>By 892 they had driven the Magyars west of the Dnieper, and their pressure made the Magyars leave Etelköz forever and settle in <b>Pannonia</b>, where they later founded the Hungarian state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
